Clean manufacturing and contamination control — in development (@ergon-history-5: 0, is #chapter) { r_005EA4AD1C9AF759E1F82587F7
Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

ERG-181 remains pending. [Clean manufacturing and contamination control](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN), predicate `clean-manufacturing`, is the developed root. Its identity is distinct from planning UID `r_47NQ213DRJK1AMKQX7DTXR8E6X`. [Production and quality](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/Production.lingua#r_2Y7YR9YHGPS7CWXQ7R4SAJVBR4) is now a real field entrance connected from Hephaestus and the register. Keep the complete particle, chemical, biological and environmental scope across microelectronics, precision manufacture, pharmaceuticals and research; the opening is not completion of that scope.

The first publication contains 45 own Records: 36 learning steps, the entrance, seven sources and a source index. Fourteen shared quantitative, chemical, fluid-pressure and metrology Records make exactly 51 selected/reachable learning Records. They retain all original imports, text and other continuations; only the new subject's assertions and visible continuations were added. No reverse imports were added. Discovery reaches the same 2,446 documents from five entries, including the existing gas composition through its oxygen-cleaning source, while the selected learning route excludes those other subjects and sources. Three investigations branch from `cm-study-entry` into sensitive-surface, air-sampling and aseptic-process studies, then rejoin at `cm-foundations-review` (`r_4Q8V901XVQ8PTKRGG5Y26MVY5N`). The transport extension below now continues from that checkpoint.

The opening begins with useful function, English–Portuguese vocabulary and process contacts. Shared arithmetic, fractions, powers and units precede size and material states. Ratios and geometry support number/mass/area comparisons, particle diameter conventions, distributions, airborne/surface quantities, area coverage and films. Biology and logarithmic growth distinguish microbial evidence, cleaning, disinfection, sterilization and preservation. Metrology supports sampling, volume/count inference, zero-count bounds, blanks, recovery, traceability, cleanliness classes and operating states. Elementary source/transport/removal balances connect deposition, mixed volumes, filtration/bypass, capture mechanisms, pressure boundaries, contact and recontamination. Each node has reading, original connective explanation and work; substantial books and chapters remain part of the progression.

Seven sources are published: NASA CR 4740 (1996, 132 PDF pages); Tschudi/Sartor/Xu LBNL-49223 (December 2001, 42 pages); DOE-HDBK-1169-2022 (267 pages); Chen/Fletcher/Cheng aerosol-instrument calibration (2011, 31-page author proof); OpenStax Microbiology (2016, current online edition); WHO TRS1044 Annex 2 (2022, 83 pages); and FDA aseptic-processing guidance (September 2004 PDF, October landing-page date, 63 pages). Existing quantitative/chemical/metrology reading and EIGA oxygen-cleaning guidance are reused. Books and media remain external links; the six downloaded reports/chapters are only in `/tmp` and the microbiology book is linked online. The source index and source Records distinguish assigned full reading from inspected passages.

Retain the source limits. NTRS identifies NASA CR 4740 as US-government work permitting public use, with component credits; its historical standards are not current requirements. The NIST-hosted calibration chapter is a Wiley author proof with unresolved queries and credited figures. DOE's official page labels its handbook Archive. Current OpenStax pages identify CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and additional notices; do not infer an older PDF's terms from today's website. WHO's catalogue for report ISBN 9789240063822 identifies CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 IGO, with component exceptions. The source metadata, hashes and precise inspection limits are in `/tmp/clean-manufacturing-source-leads.json`. ECSS-Q-ST-70-01C Revision 1 dated 15 October 2025 was located, but the current official page/PDF redirects to an ESA outage page; full text and rights remain uninspected. Do not substitute the earlier 2024 draft as a final standard.

Opening checks passed: 51 independent dimensional, geometric, statistical, transient-balance and recurrence comparisons in `/tmp/check-clean-opening-physics.py`. These check SI scale conversions, sphere mass and projected area, film thickness, repeated doubling, count/volume uncertainty, Poisson zero-count bounds with known recovery, deposition flux, bypass flow conservation, mixed-volume transients against independent RK4 and integrated inventory balances, and exact rational cleaning recurrences. They verify the declared teaching models, not real manufacturing performance. No new embedded program was added in this opening.

Static publication checks passed in `/tmp/audit-clean-opening.py`: all 45 own files contain one Record, exact 51-Record selection/reachability, five-entry discovery, source exclusion, three-way investigation branching, fourteen shared Records preserved byte-for-byte after removing only the new navigation, and no source media or views directory. The Hephaestus parser passed for 2,594 files and 2,632 Records; the structural audit finds 2,592 unique explicit identities and seven developed field entrances. All 31,621 local Lingua references and 237 execution-ledger links resolve. The final register parser and whitespace checks passed. The register remains 261 Records, sixteen Hephaestus groups, 134 pending subjects and 182 historical pending scopes across umbrellas. The new opening has not yet had its own runtime export/relocation test; run that after the full subject is authored. Gas publication passed before shared clean-manufacturing continuations were added, and the new shared-content audit verifies that those additions preserve its existing content and route.

Authoring artifacts: `/tmp/clean-opening-draft.py`, `/tmp/clean-opening-data.json`, `/tmp/publish-clean-opening.py`, `/tmp/clean-opening-publication.json`, `/tmp/clean-opening-shared-before.json`, `/tmp/clean-opening-before.json` and `/tmp/clean-opening-audit-result.json`. The publisher has run successfully. Do not rerun it over existing content. Extend the existing files and root identity. The previous gas runtime result is `/tmp/gases-publication-result.json`: one passing test in 1827.78 seconds, process exited; no gas process remains pending.

Transport publication adds 36 learning Records, four sources and thirteen shared steps. The subject now has 85 own Records: 73 learning Records including the root, eleven sources and an index. Twenty-seven shared steps give exactly 100 selected/reachable Records. Reused vectors, derivatives and accumulation Records make the transition into particle motion and spatial transport more gradual. Discovery reaches 2,486 documents from five entrances; all six investigations retain their two three-way branches and joins. The current terminal is `cm-transport-review`, [Review transport before changing the surface or facility](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/studies/Review-transport-before-changing-the-surface-or-facility.lingua). Keep this identity and extend its outgoing assertion rather than replacing the root or its prior sequence.

The extension covers particle/fluid frames and scales, isolated-sphere drag, continuum/slip regimes, explicit Knudsen conventions, measured mobility and calibration scope, relaxation/stopping distance, buoyant settling, Stokes-number scaling, nonspherical and near-wall resistance, Brownian displacement, Einstein response/diffusion, finite-time velocity correlation, electric drift and the need for separate models of other forces. Relative diffusion leads to collision kernels, unordered-pair counting, population/moment balances and limits of simple coagulation. Conservative spatial transport connects absorbing/reflecting/capture boundaries, near-wall resistance, collectors and turbulence to an original numerical example. The final studies investigate mobility inference, collecting surfaces and number/material population dynamics. Quantitative specialist thermal/acoustic/optical migration still needs application-specific primary evidence during the later specialist work; the present Record introduces and assigns that comparison without claiming a supplied universal force model.

New source Records link Ahmadi's ME537 aerosol course (eight locally inspected PDFs total 72 pages), Mei's eight-page MIT aerosol/coagulation notes, Kim/Mulholland/Kukuck/Pui's 24-page 2005 slip-correction study and Kulkarni/Baron/Willeke's sixteen-page single-particle transport chapter. All remain external. Clarkson's course and PDFs are undated; a 2020 upload path is not a publication date, some headings lack downloads and course labels differ between files. Selected drag/slip, Brownian and coagulation pages were viewed visually, including missing glyphs in the coagulation PDF. No blanket adaptation licence was verified. MIT's page-2 continuum inequality is reversed relative to NIST and the second course; its pair-kernel/total-count equations omit a half factor that is explicit in the independent event count and Ahmadi's equation. These source discrepancies are explained in learning Records and not silently copied. NIST's catalogue renders the first author's name differently from the PDF; the citation follows the PDF. Its metadata and introductory scientific passages were read through the web reader, while local retrieval returned 403 and the full experiment was not reproduced. The CDC chapter retains its earlier limited inspection and conflicting catalogue/PDF rights notices. Full reading is assigned without claiming full author reproduction.

The original embedded `particle_column.py` solves a bounded one-dimensional settling/diffusion model with cell averages, downward upwind transport, centered interior diffusion, collecting bottom and zero total top flux. It accumulates the same bottom flux in a deposited inventory and rejects time steps outside its nonnegative-coefficient bound. It includes no coagulation, growth, electric or thermal migration, wall release or real facility geometry. The default derives diffusion and settling from one hypothetical particle mobility and reports 10,000 initial particles/m², approximately 2961.68933 remaining and 7038.31067 collected after 200 seconds. These are discrete teaching results, not a measured or qualified process. The source block was extracted and run from `/tmp`; its SHA-256 is `23dc331ef00e17a06b393d35371d47ab27d433f5891e25128f5cb8bc34fce9c6`.

Checks passed in `/tmp/check-clean-column.py`: 350 independent comparisons against exact binomial finite-step advection, exact discrete diffusion eigenmodes, continuum diffusion refinement with consistent space/time scaling, sharp-front advection refinement, inventory and dimensional scaling; thirteen invalid domains rejected. Diffusion errors on 12/24/48/96 cells were approximately 1.39017e−5, 3.47332e−6, 8.68198e−7 and 2.17041e−7. Sharp-front errors on 20/40/80 cells decreased separately as expected for the discontinuous profile. `/tmp/check-clean-transport-physics.py` passed 52 independent high-precision mobility/settling calculations, Brownian-correlation quadrature, transient-motion integrations, exact unordered-pair counts and population-moment balances. These complement the opening's earlier 51 comparisons. The rendered program exactly matches the tested source and ran successfully in a fresh process outside the repository.

Publication checks passed in `/tmp/audit-clean-transport.py`: 85 one-Record files, exact 100-Record selection/reachability, five-entry discovery of 2,486 documents, both investigation branches, source exclusion, twenty-seven shared Records preserved byte-for-byte after removing only new navigation, original program identity and no bundled media/views. The Hephaestus parser passed for 2,634 files and 2,672 Records; there are 2,632 unique explicit developed identities. All 31,986 local Lingua references resolve after the three mathematical bridges were added. Seven field entrances and the 261-Record register remain connected, with sixteen groups, 134 pending Hephaestus subjects and 182 historical pending scopes. The final register parser, 238 ledger links and whitespace checks passed before the surface extension below. The full subject's runtime export/relocation test remains pending until its remaining content is authored.

Artifacts for this published extension: `/tmp/clean-transport-draft.py`, `/tmp/clean-transport-data.json`, `/tmp/publish-clean-transport.py`, `/tmp/clean-transport-publication.json`, `/tmp/clean-transport-shared-before.json`, `/tmp/clean-transport-before.json`, `/tmp/clean-transport-audit-result.json`, `/tmp/cm_column.py` and `/tmp/clean-rendered-column.py`. The publisher has run; do not rerun it over authored files. Course downloads and their hashes are in `/tmp/cm-transport-primary-downloads.json`. The earlier opening audit has historical counts and is superseded by the combined transport audit.


Adhesion and surface-state publication adds 26 learning Records, three source Records and six reused foundations. The subject now has 114 own Records: 99 learning Records including its entrance, fourteen sources and an index. Thirty-three shared steps give exactly 132 selected/reachable learning Records. Discovery reaches 2,515 documents from seven entrances. The three contact, wetting and retention investigations branch from `cm-adhesion-studies` and rejoin at `cm-adhesion-review`, [Review surfaces before choosing cleaning chemistry](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/studies/Review-surfaces-before-choosing-cleaning-chemistry.lingua). This is the new terminal; `cm-transport-review` now continues through [Read surfaces from contact history to measured removal](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/surfaces/Read-surfaces-from-contact-history-to-measured-removal.lingua). Preserve both identities and the previous sequence.

The progression covers surface composition, geometry and history; reversible interfacial work; the ideal sphere–plane dispersion potential, force and medium dependence; effective-modulus/radius conventions; JKR and DMT contact, loading control and interaction-range limits; roughness across scales; ideal image charge and localized-charge limits; wetting, hysteresis, Wenzel/Cassie states, capillary bridges; rolling/sliding/lifting; instrument and protocol effects on adhesion measurements; population removal inference; coverage kinetics; gas/surface reservoirs; and the distinction between amount, state and restored function. Six shared Records (`gc-interfaces`, `th-interfaces`, `mech-contact`, `fd-wetting`, `gas-residence`, `gas-readsorption`) retain their original text/imports/routes and add only the new subject's directed assertions and visible continuation. No reverse discovery imports were added.

Sources: Rimai's two particle–substrate presentations (12 and 11 pages) preserve his authorship and Ahmadi's editing credit. Four further Ahmadi PDFs on dispersion, JKR/DMT, rough particles and bumpy particles (7, 7, 6 and 10 pages) extend the existing course source. Selected model/measurement passages were read; dispersion page 4, adhesion-model pages 1 and 3, and Rimai part 1 page 7 were viewed visually. Some displayed symbols are missing and extraction drops radicals. These notes have no verified blanket reuse licence. Pastewka and Robbins' arXiv:1311.1178v1 (5 November 2013, 26 pages including supplement) adds a research comparison of rough contacts: early derivation, methods and selected interaction-range passages were inspected, but simulations, figures and the full supplement were not reproduced. The arXiv non-exclusive licence grants distribution rights to the archive, not general adaptation rights. Its 2014 PNAS journal edition remains a distinct version. Kim and colleagues' 2008 humid-air adhesion paper is a bounded research lead: NIST metadata and abstract were inspected, browser PDF access failed and local retrieval returned 403. The source and study explicitly require full methods/results before claiming experimental reproduction.

The existing Bush MIT interfacial-phenomena source is reused. Lectures 3, 4 and 15 were downloaded only to `/tmp`; the wetting/energy/hysteresis text was inspected and lecture 15 page 4 was viewed visually. Its crossover prose reverses the preceding inequality. The authored comparison derives the free-energy difference per unit projected area eW−eC=−γ[(r−f)cos θ+(1−f)] and separates lower equilibrium energy from an actual transition or metastable state. The shared source Record and fluid-dynamics content are preserved. Neither the notes' simplified state-switching claims nor their transcribed equations are copied without assumptions and checks.

Independent calculations passed in `/tmp/check-clean-adhesion-physics.py`: 251 comparisons covering 70-digit finite-difference checks and independent quadrature of the sphere–plane force/potential, contact-load inversion and numerical minimization of the JKR pull-off, Hertz/modulus convention conversion, DMT ratios, approximate medium arithmetic, direct interface-energy sums and exact rational wetting inequalities, capillary force scaling, image-charge energy/force, numerical integration of coverage and reservoir dynamics, and equal-rms-height profiles with distinct slopes. The initial wetting sign comparison encountered a floating-point ambiguity exactly at equal energies; rational arithmetic now checks that boundary rather than classifying numerical roundoff as a physical preference. For the hypothetical JKR case R=0.5 µm, W=0.05 J/m² and E*=1 GPa, a0≈56.1165 nm and pull-off≈117.810 nN. These verify the stated teaching models, not real contact or cleaning performance. No new embedded program was added; the original particle-column program and hash remain unchanged.

Combined publication audit `/tmp/audit-clean-adhesion.py` passed: 114 one-Record files, exact 132-Record directed selection/reachability, seven-entry discovery, nine investigations in three branches/joins, fourteen source Records excluded, thirty-three shared Records preserved byte-for-byte after removing only new navigation, unchanged original inline program, no source media and no views directory. The Hephaestus parser passed for 2,663 files and 2,701 Records. There are 2,661 unique explicit identities and all 32,236 local Lingua references resolve. The register remains 261 Records, sixteen groups, 134 pending Hephaestus subjects, seven developed field entrances and 182 historical pending scopes across umbrellas. The final register parser, all 240 execution-ledger links and whitespace checks passed. Runtime export/relocation for the whole clean-manufacturing subject remains pending until its remaining content is authored.

Artifacts: `/tmp/clean-adhesion-draft.py`, `/tmp/clean-adhesion-data.json`, `/tmp/publish-clean-adhesion.py`, `/tmp/clean-adhesion-publication.json`, `/tmp/clean-adhesion-shared-before.json`, `/tmp/clean-adhesion-before.json`, `/tmp/audit-clean-adhesion.py`, `/tmp/clean-adhesion-audit-result.json` and `/tmp/check-clean-adhesion-physics.py`. The publisher has run; never rerun it over existing files. The earlier transport audit has historical counts and is superseded by the combined adhesion audit. The mathematical-bridge refinement is recorded in `/tmp/refine-clean-foundation-bridges.py` and `/tmp/clean-foundation-bridges-before.json`; its three shared snapshots were appended to the transport preservation file. New PDF hashes/page counts and failed humidity retrieval are in `/tmp/cm-new-surface-downloads.json`; the six earlier Clarkson files remain in `/tmp/cm-surface-downloads.json`.


Contamination chemistry and cleaning adds 36 learning Records, five source Records and nine reused foundations. The subject now contains 155 own Records: 135 learning Records including the entrance, nineteen sources and an index. Forty-two shared learning Records give exactly 177 selected/reachable Records. The residue, rinse and sensitive-manufacturing investigations branch from `cm-cleaning-studies` and rejoin at `cm-cleaning-review`, [Review cleaning before designing the facility and its controls](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/studies/Review-cleaning-before-designing-the-facility-and-its-controls.lingua). This is the next attachment point. The preceding `cm-adhesion-review` now leads to [Read cleaning through chemistry, transport and restored function](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/cleaning/Read-cleaning-through-chemistry-transport-and-restored-function.lingua); preserve the existing identities and progression.

The new material develops residue identity and conserved components; operational definitions of NVR; aliquots, blanks, extraction recovery and area; chemical identification versus amount; finite dissolution capacity and rate; acid/base state, complexation and precipitation; surfactant populations; ionic screening, a bounded DLVO potential and zeta inference; substrate/coating/bath compatibility; solvent, supercritical, aqueous, acoustic, dry and UV/ozone cleaning; particle-map occupancy; batch/continuous rinses and slow regions; drying inventory, patterns and capillary effects; airborne molecular contamination; and handling/storage preservation. Three investigations require full relevant readings, independently reconstructed calculations and criticism of removal versus retained function. These extend the formation rather than completing every specialist manufacturing or lifecycle question.

The shared steps are `gc-preparation`, `gc-solution-interactions`, `gc-acidbase`, `gc-ph`, `gc-solubility`, `gc-complexes`, `gc-phases`, `fd-cavitation-dynamics` and `wu-high-purity`. Their original bodies, discovery imports and subject assertions remain byte-exact after removing only the new clean-manufacturing assertions and visible continuations. Existing shared content from all earlier extensions also passed that preservation check. No reverse imports or new application feature were added. The original particle-column program remains unchanged at SHA-256 `23dc331ef00e17a06b393d35371d47ab27d433f5891e25128f5cb8bc34fce9c6`.

Five external primary sources support this section. Taborelli's nineteen-page arXiv:2006.01585v1 is a 2020 submission of a 2017 CERN-school contribution; its selected cleaning, analysis, evaluation and packaging passages were inspected, with page 4 viewed. Its supercritical-fluid prose confuses the triple and critical points; the Record uses the correct phase distinction. The archive's non-exclusive distribution licence is not general adaptation permission. Gordon, Rutherford and Aranda's NASA/TM-2017-219591 is a 111-page main report with instrument appendices. Collection, gravimetry, initial identification discussion and conclusion passages were inspected; cover and printed page 4 were viewed. The official catalogue supplies the number obscured by the cover font. Its ungrouped mass/blank/volume expression is dimensionally ambiguous; the authored balance is independently derived. NTRS permits public use of the US-government work while component credits remain relevant. Full appendices, spectra and experiments are not claimed reproduced.

Johannsmann's 86-page institutional *Physical Chemistry of Interfaces and Colloids* SS26 file supplies substantial adsorption, surfactant, screening, electrokinetic, stability and drying reading. Selected passages were inspected and page 68 viewed to check radicals and SI concentration units; no blanket adaptation licence was verified. ECS Proceedings Volume 2003-26, *Cleaning Technology in Semiconductor Device Manufacturing VIII*, was published in 2004 after the 2003 symposium. Its 448-page publisher PDF is freely readable but all rights reserved. Contents and selected method/result passages from Chang and colleagues' printed pages 27–33 and Vereecke and colleagues' 145–152 were inspected. Other full articles are assigned reading, not claimed independently reproduced; historical process nodes are not present specifications. Hirano and colleagues' 2013 seven-page MEMS particle-removal study, DOI 10.1541/ieejsmas.133.157, has Japanese main text and English abstract/captions. Selected methods/counting passages were inspected, including a visual check of equation 1 on printed page 158. It is paired with the English proceedings and remains external under IEEJ copyright. No full experimental or plotted-result reproduction is claimed. All PDFs and extracted text remain under `/tmp`, outside the repository.

Independent verification in `/tmp/check-clean-chemistry-physics.py` passed 110 comparisons plus conservation, physical-root, state-bound and limiting-case assertions. It uses exact fractions, 65-digit Decimal arithmetic, enumeration of small occupancy experiments, independent bisection, finite differences and RK4 integration. Checks cover NVR/area conversion, dissolution inventory/transients, acid and ligand equilibria, surfactant inventory, number-density versus molar screening expressions, linearization, zeta units/sign, DLVO extrema, occupancy saturation/inversion, batch waste closure, continuous rinse discharge, two-volume eigenmodes and conserved inventory, drying residue and transport times. The NVR example gives 0.4375 µg/cm²; the screening example gives 9.61370 nm; the illustrative potential's maximum/minimum are at x≈0.253871/6.841848 with energies 23.15366/−0.24959 kBT. The 1024-cell particle map gives expected occupied counts 48.82219 and 1023.94151 for 50 and 10,000 independently located particles. In the two-volume example, after 100 minutes the flushed/slow concentrations are approximately 0.004565/0.412262 times their common initial value. These establish the explicit teaching models, not a real process or apparatus performance.

Combined static audit `/tmp/audit-clean-chemistry.py` passed: 155 one-Record files; exactly 177 selected and directed-reachable learning Records; nineteen source Records excluded; forty-two shared Records preserved; twelve investigations in four branches/joins; unchanged inline program; no bundled media or views directory. Nine entrances discover the same 2,905 documents. The first count expectation incorrectly assumed every shared source was already discovered. Comparing against the saved previous root established 2,515 prior documents, 41 new files and 349 existing dependencies introduced by `wu-high-purity`: 170 water/sanitation and 179 electrical-power files. Only the intended high-purity-water step from that added dependency set joins the selected route. Discovery and selection remain distinct; no existing route was broadened by copying its content or predicates.

The Hephaestus parser passed for 2,704 files containing 2,742 Records; the 261-Record development register also parses. There are 2,702 unique explicit developed Hephaestus identities, sixteen planning groups, seven developed field entrances, 134 pending Hephaestus subjects and 182 historical scopes across umbrellas. All 32,592 local Lingua references resolve. Register and umbrella summaries now describe the cleaning extension while ERG-181 remains pending. All 242 execution-ledger links and whitespace checks passed. A final control-volume wording refinement separates net species inflow, gas inventory, uptake and reaction. Checking that file alone could not resolve its shared `@clean-manufacturing` slug because the CLI validates the supplied file set; the complete Hephaestus check passed again afterward. Runtime publication/export/relocation for the complete clean-manufacturing subject remains deferred until the remaining formation is authored; this extension changes learning data only.

Artifacts are `/tmp/clean-chemistry-draft.py`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-data.json`, `/tmp/publish-clean-chemistry.py`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-publication.json`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-before.json`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-shared-before.json`, `/tmp/check-clean-chemistry-physics.py`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-physics-result.json`, `/tmp/audit-clean-chemistry.py`, `/tmp/clean-chemistry-audit-result.json` and `/tmp/clean-chemistry-discovery-delta.json`. The publisher has run; do not rerun it or the draft generator over authored Records. Earlier audits contain historical counts. Source PDF hashes/page counts are in `/tmp/cm-chemistry-source-leads.json` and `/tmp/cm-cleaning-extension-downloads.json`; the former's early metadata-only inspection status is superseded by the inspection recorded here and in the new source Records.


The facility extension adds 47 learning Records, three source Records and eight reused foundations. The subject now contains 205 own Records: 182 learning Records including its entrance, twenty-two sources and an index. Fifty shared learning Records give exactly 232 selected/reachable Records. Three new investigations of installed air cleaning, pressure/transfer and facility energy/control branch from cm-facility-studies and rejoin at cm-facility-review, [Review the facility before qualifying its performance](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/studies/Review-the-facility-before-qualifying-its-performance.lingua). This facility checkpoint now continues into aerosol measurement. The previous cm-cleaning-review now continues into [Read the facility as a connected physical system](../../ergon/hephaestus/production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/facilities/Read-the-facility-as-a-connected-physical-system.lingua). Existing roots and identities are preserved.

The formation develops process-derived requirements; people/material/product/waste paths; protection versus containment; local barriers and access states; personnel/process sources; garments and handling; envelope materials and penetrations; air circuits and quantity bases; room recovery and local transport; species-specific recirculation; number/mass filter efficiencies; depth-survival models; bypass inference and identifiability; staged/common-path filtration; loading and fan operating points; integrated energy; pressure references, network balances and a worked two-room solution; upstream-directed contaminant transport; buoyancy, wind, doorway counterflows and moving boundaries; airlock states; observations and model validation; thermal/humidity balances; effective room capacity; utility interfaces and common dependencies; sensor placement, averaging and response; demand control and recovery; service access; construction/handover; and three substantial investigations.

Eight shared Records are fd-networks, fd-hydrostatics, fd-buoyant, th-control, gas-humidity-definitions, th-lumped, cs-common-cause and mech-human-access. Their original bodies, discovery imports and routes remain byte-exact after removing only the added clean-manufacturing navigation. Earlier shared Records from all four preceding extensions pass the same preservation check. The earlier room and filter-balance Records are referenced for their introductory models; the facility section adds operating states, inverse inference, connected networks and independent evidence. No reverse imports, special Trail feature, views directory or bundled source media were added.

New primary readings: Dols and Polidoro's CONTAM User Guide and Program Documentation, Version 3.4, NIST TN 1887 Revision 1 (August 2020), 340 PDF pages; Faulkner, Fisk and Walton's Energy Savings in Cleanrooms from Demand-Controlled Filtration, LBNL-38869 (1996), thirteen-page manuscript; and Zheng and colleagues' Aerosol exchange between pressure-equilibrium rooms induced by door motion and human movement, Building and Environment 241, 110486 (2023), DOI 10.1016/j.buildenv.2023.110486, inspected through free PMC full-text HTML. The two PDFs remain in /tmp; hashes/page counts are in /tmp/cm-facility-downloads.json.

CONTAM's selected zone/path, mixture, contaminant, numerical and airflow-theory passages were inspected. PDF pages 263 and 286 (printed 251 and 274) were viewed visually because text extraction omitted equations. NIST describes its software as public domain with attribution/modification notices requested; dependencies including SUNDIALS carry separate terms. No blanket rights over every component are inferred. The 2020 documentation edition is fixed; the official site offers later software revisions. No CONTAM executable, tutorial project or complete model was run here. Teaching balances are independently checked rather than claimed as application reproduction.

The demand-control paper's methods, instrumentation, controls, energy and concentration passages were read; manuscript page 6's table was viewed. It records two baseline powers, different counter sampling/reporting intervals, one-minute stored averages without instantaneous extremes, localized contamination, excursions and an accidentally disabled fan. Its energy comparison is retained with these limits. Free institutional reading was verified; general adaptation permission was not established. Raw data and the whole experiment were not reproduced.

The door-motion paper's selected methods/validation passages and data statement were inspected. Its moving-mesh CFD comparison acknowledges a hinge-geometry omission and an early plume discrepancy in a water-chamber experiment. The hospital-room application is not treated as proof for a manufacturing airlock. Full figures, meshes, raw data and simulations were not reproduced; data are offered on request. Publisher access returned 403 and the PMC PDF link returned a preparation page; HTML supplied the inspected text. PMC states author copyright and a conditional Elsevier COVID research-reuse permission. No unrestricted component adaptation is claimed; full reading remains assigned.

The existing LBNL-49223 source now records further inspection of programming, loads, contamination requirements, thermal/utility planning, climate and layout. DOE-HDBK-1169-2022 records further system/zoning, filter mechanisms/resistance, installed testing, fan/system, intake and control inspection. Its official page still labels it Archive and distinguishes it from the companion standard. Historical numerical overviews, application-specific limits and old standard references were not adopted as universal inputs. Full figures, prescribed tests and facilities have not been independently reproduced.

Independent verification in /tmp/check-clean-facilities-physics.py passed 112 comparisons plus exact conservation, bounds and limiting-case assertions. Exact fractions, 65-digit Decimal arithmetic, independent bisection and iterative linear solution, finite differences, quadrature and RK4 integration check room transients/inventory; number/mass efficiencies; depth capture; bypass inference/sensitivity; staged/common paths; fan loading and energy scaling; network pressures, reversals, upstream transport and summed inventories; wind and doorway counterflow; airlock recovery; heat/humidity bases; thermal response; averaging and cascaded room/sensor response; and historical energy baselines. A draft decimal error was corrected before publication: b=0.001 and Pm=0.0001 give Psystem=0.0010999, not 0.001099.

Selected checked results: room asymptote 22.177419 particles/m³, time constant 20.161290 s and 99%-gap recovery 92.846173 s; mixed-size efficiency 0.745 by number and 0.98951049 by mass; initial fan flow 4.342585 m³/s at 622.839 Pa, with doubled linear resistance giving 3.333333 m³/s at fixed speed or requiring relative speed 1.197605 to preserve flow; two-room pressures 9.677419/3.548387 Pa and concentrations 20/23.263158 particles/m³; approximately 0.248836 m³/s in each direction through the symmetric Boussinesq doorway despite zero net volume flow; and moisture removal 0.006411049 kg/s per 1 kg/s dry air. These establish bounded teaching models, not installed process performance. No new inline program was added; the existing particle-column code/hash remains unchanged.

