The formation develops population and observation boundaries; sample volume and gas-state conversion; aspiration, inlet matching, line losses and delay; conditioning and material changes; optical response, thresholds and nested-channel covariance; condensation counting and size efficiency; distinct coincidence models and inverse branches; aerodynamic equivalence; mobility, charge distributions and scanning; response matrices, weak identifiability and inverse assumptions; counting precision, zero-count limits and background subtraction; calibration design, electrometer reference chains, splitter swaps and dilution tests; uncertainty budgets; and comparisons of complete measuring systems. The three investigations require forward models, independent checks, competing explanations and a conclusion tied to the actual process question.

