New primary readings are Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie's CPC proportionality calibration, a seventeen-page manuscript supplied by NIST, DOI 10.1080/02786826.2011.637998, and Wiedensohler and colleagues' Mobility particle size spectrometers, Atmospheric Measurement Techniques 5, 657–685 (2012), DOI 10.5194/amt-5-657-2012, twenty-nine pages. The former's catalogue date is November 2011; its catalogue bibliographic fields are not treated as a checked final publisher edition. Free institutional reading was verified without a blanket adaptation licence. The latter carries CC BY 3.0 with component credits retained. Both PDFs remain in /tmp, with hashes and page counts in /tmp/cm-aerosol-measurement-downloads.json.

For the CPC manuscript, apparatus, dilution, coincidence correction, primary calibration, uncertainty and splitter passages were inspected. PDF pages 4 and 16 were viewed to recover equations lost by text extraction. The authored splitter derivation declares its own ratio orientation rather than silently copying ambiguous extracted symbols. For the mobility paper, instrument-principle, flow/state, loss-correction, inversion-comparison and efficiency passages were inspected. Complete experiments and original datasets were not independently reproduced. Full readings remain assigned. The existing Chen/Fletcher/Cheng source now records deeper inspection of equivalent diameters, optical/mobility response, CPC calibration and flow/mass passages; it remains an author proof with extraction and edition limits. Instrument ranges and historical documentary references were not presented as universal current requirements.

Independent verification passed 74 comparisons plus domain and conservation assertions. Exact fractions, 65-digit Decimal arithmetic, independent integration and Gaussian elimination, bracketed inversion, probability summation, finite differences and a seeded million-arrival renewal simulation verify the teaching calculations. Checked examples include the 10.782 L reference volume; tube penetrations 0.9273741104 and 0.4704892177 with 0.753982 s transit estimate; distinct dead-time responses and two paralyzable inverse branches; aerodynamic equivalence and DMA mobility 1.8386300013e-7 m²/(V·s); stable and weak response-matrix inversions; Poisson moments, nested-bin covariance, background variance and zero-count inference; electrometer current 2.67029439 fA; splitter/gain separation; dilution offsets; and a relative uncertainty of 11.7473401%. The ideal zero-count upper limit is 0.0374466534 L⁻¹ for the stated 100 L sample and 0.8 efficiency. These verify bounded teaching models, not instrument or facility performance. A printed-rounding tolerance in the temporary checker was corrected; no published numerical error was found.

